  "title": "The Hard Problem of Consciousness",
  "emoji": "💭",
  "color": "#581C87",
  "description": "A fast breakdown of The Hard Problem of Consciousness for builders.",
  "is_free": true,
  "published": true,
  "story": {
    "hook": {
      "text": "Science can explain which brain regions activate when you see red. It cannot explain why seeing red feels like anything at all.",
      "visual": "🔴"
    },
    "buildup": {
      "text": "David Chalmers coined the hard problem in 1995 to distinguish it from the easy problems of explaining behavior and cognition.",
      "visual": "📖"
    },
    "discovery": {
      "text": "The hard problem asks why any of this is accompanied by subjective experience.",
      "visual": "❓"
    },
    "twist": {
      "text": "Even a complete map of every neuron and synapse in your brain would not explain why there is something it is like to be you.",
      "visual": "🗺️"
    },
    "climax": {
      "text": "Others argue it will dissolve once we understand the brain well enough.",
      "visual": "⚔️"
    },
    "punchline": {
      "text": "We can explain how the brain works.",
      "visual": "🌌"
    }
  },
  "quiz": {
    "question": "What is the hard problem of consciousness?",
    "options": [
      "Why brain processes are accompanied by subjective experience",
      "How to build a brain from scratch",
      "Why some neurons fire faster than others"
    ],
    "correct": 0
  },
